The '''Unió d'Artesans del Cinema''' (UAC) is the trade union and awards body of [[Cinema of Balboa|Balboan cinema]]. Founded in 1976 by actors in response to the consolidation of the film industry following the [[Treaty of Areza]] and the perceived exploitation of creative workers by the post-reunification studios, the UAC originally represented actors only. In 1982, the union expanded its membership to include directors, writers, technicians, and producers, becoming the comprehensive trade union of the Balboan film industry. The following year, the UAC established the annual UAC Awards ({{lang|ca|Premis UAC}}), the principal cinema awards in Balboa, which recognise artistic and technical achievement in Balboan filmmaking.

== History ==

=== Founding ===

The UAC was established in 1976, three years after the [[Treaty of Areza]] and the reunification of the canal corridor with Balboa. The consolidation of the Silver age's fragmented, low-budget studios into fewer, larger, better-capitalised entities — a process that had begun in the late 1960s and was completed by the reunification — transformed the power dynamic between the studios and the creative workers. The consolidated studios had greater bargaining power, standardised employment contracts that favoured the employers, and reduced the opportunities for actors to move between studios in search of better conditions. The actors who formed the UAC — led by [[Lluís Homar i Sastre]], a Volisanian-language stage and film actor who had worked with [[Estudis del Port]] since the late Silver age — argued that the consolidated studios were exploiting the post-reunification labour market to depress wages and to impose working conditions that would not have been tolerated in the pre-consolidation industry.

The founding meeting, held in the back room of a bar in the harbour-front district of [[Sant Cristòfor]], was attended by approximately forty actors. The early UAC was a small, informal organisation: it had no permanent office, no full-time staff, and no formal recognition from the studios. Its activities consisted primarily of coordinating salary demands, sharing information about studio practices, and providing mutual support for actors in disputes with management. The UAC's early membership was drawn primarily from the Volisanian-language studios, particularly Estudis del Port, where the consolidation had been most disruptive to the pre-existing labour practices.

The studios initially refused to recognise the UAC. The post-reunification studios, which were in the process of establishing their commercial operations in the unified Balboan market, regarded the union as an illegitimate interference in their employment practices and argued that the actors were independent contractors rather than employees. The UAC's position was that the actors were economically dependent on the studios — the studios controlled the production facilities, the distribution networks, and the theatre chains — and that the actors' nominal status as independent contractors was a legal fiction that allowed the studios to avoid the obligations of employers. The dispute over recognition was the defining conflict of the UAC's early years.

=== Actors' union era (1976–1982) ===

The UAC's early campaigns focused on the issues that most directly affected actors: the standardisation of employment contracts, the regulation of working hours, the payment of residuals for repeat screenings, and the protection of actors from arbitrary dismissal. The union's first significant victory was the 1978 Studio Agreement, in which [[Estudis del Port]] became the first major studio to recognise the UAC as the representative of its actors and to agree to a standardised contract that included minimum fees, overtime provisions, and residual payments. The agreement was followed by similar agreements with the other major studios, and by 1980, the UAC was recognised by all of the principal mainstream studios.

The [[Balboa real-estate bubble|real-estate bubble]] of 1978–1987 transformed the economic context in which the UAC operated. The surge of capital into the film industry produced larger budgets and higher salaries for the most prominent actors, but the bubble also widened the gap between the star performers, who commanded premium fees, and the working actors, who were employed on the standardised contracts that the UAC had negotiated. The studios' growing reliance on star power — a central feature of the bubble-era production model — created a two-tier labour market in which a small number of actors earned disproportionately high fees while the majority of UAC members saw little benefit from the boom. The UAC's leadership argued that the two-tier system undermined the solidarity of the union and that the studios were using the star system to divide the workforce.

The bubble also produced the conditions that led to the UAC's expansion. The studios' growing budgets and their investment in production values created demand for skilled technicians — cinematographers, editors, sound engineers, production designers — whose working conditions were unregulated and whose wages had not kept pace with the rising costs of living in Sant Cristòfor. The directors and writers, who were nominally the creative leaders of the industry, were increasingly treated as employees of the studios rather than as independent creative artists, and their creative control was being eroded by the commercial pressures of the bubble-era production model. The producers, who managed the studios' production schedules and budgets, were caught between the demands of the studio management and the creative personnel, and their working conditions were deteriorating as the studios' expectations increased.

=== Expansion (1982) ===

In 1982, the UAC expanded its membership to include directors, writers, technicians, and producers. The expansion was driven by the recognition that the actors' union could not effectively represent the interests of the film industry's creative workers without the participation of the other professional categories, and by the growing demand from non-actor professionals for representation. The expansion was controversial within the UAC's membership: some actors argued that the union's focus would be diluted by the inclusion of other professional categories, and that the actors' specific concerns would be neglected in a broader organisation. The leadership, led by Lluís Homar i Sastre, argued that the expansion was necessary to create a unified front against the studios' exploitation of all creative workers and that the actors' interests were best served by a union that represented the entire industry.

The expansion transformed the UAC from a small, actors-only organisation into a comprehensive trade union. The membership grew from approximately 300 actors in 1982 to over 1,000 members by 1985, as directors, writers, technicians, and producers joined the union. The UAC established professional sections for each category, each with its own representation in the union's governance, and negotiated separate collective agreements with the studios for each professional category. The expanded UAC also established a permanent office in the harbour-front district and hired full-time staff to manage the union's activities.

=== Awards body (1983–present) ===

In 1983, one year after the expansion, the UAC established the annual UAC Awards ({{lang|ca|Premis UAC}}). The decision to create an awards ceremony was driven by several factors. The union's leadership recognised that the awards would raise the public profile of the UAC and strengthen its position in negotiations with the studios. The awards also served a professional purpose: by recognising artistic and technical achievement, the UAC asserted that the creative workers were not merely employees but artists whose work had cultural value, and that the studios' commercial exploitation of that work should be constrained by professional standards. The awards were also a response to the bubble-era culture of excess, in which the studios' commercial success was celebrated while the creative workers who produced the films received little public recognition.

The first UAC Awards ceremony was held in 1983, recognising films released in the preceding year. The early awards were modest in scale — the ceremony was held in a converted warehouse in the harbour-front district, and the awards themselves were simple engraved plaques — but they established the principle of professional recognition for Balboan cinema. The initial categories were Best Picture, Best Director, Best Actor, Best Actress, and Best Screenplay, reflecting the professional sections of the expanded union.

The awards ceremony grew in scale and prominence during the bubble years. The ceremony was moved to the [[Auditori de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1986, a larger venue that reflected the growing scale of the industry and the increasing public attention that the awards attracted. The categories were expanded to include Best Supporting Actor, Best Supporting Actress, Best Cinematography, Best Editing, and Best Original Score in 1985, and Best Documentary in 1990.

The UAC Awards were shaped by the tension between the union's role as a labour organisation and its role as a cultural institution. The awards were voted on by the UAC's membership — the creative workers themselves — and the results reflected the professional community's assessment of artistic achievement rather than the studios' commercial priorities. This sometimes produced controversial outcomes: the popular comedies of [[Pere Bosch i Vargas]], which were commercially dominant, were regarded by the UAC's membership as less artistically significant than the satirical films of [[Jordi Molins i Castell]], and the awards reflected this hierarchy. The tension between commercial success and artistic recognition was a recurring subject of commentary in the Balboan press.

The underground film culture of the Gilded age was not represented within the UAC. The underground's filmmakers — [[Montserrat Riera i Sola]], [[Adrià Pons i Riera]], [[Kwame Rovira]] — were not UAC members, as they worked outside the mainstream studio system, and their films were not eligible for the awards, which required distribution through the mainstream theatre system. The UAC's exclusion of the underground was a source of criticism from the Sant Cristòfor cultural press, particularly after the ''[[El Desig (film)|El Desig]]'' [[Balboa v. Riera i Sola|obscenity trial of 1988]]. The UAC did not take an official position on the trial, but several individual members, including Jordi Molins i Castell, publicly supported the defence.

The [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis|asset crisis]] of 1985–1991 and the subsequent foreign acquisition of the major studios placed the UAC in a difficult position. The foreign-owned studios that emerged from the consolidation — [[Estudis Sant Cristòfor]] (ESC) and [[Pellícules i Televisió de Balboa]] (PTB) — were controlled by Volisanian and Zong parent companies, and the UAC's collective agreements were complicated by the presence of foreign ownership. The UAC advocated for the preservation of domestic creative control and for the protection of Balboan employment in the industry, and its position as an independent trade union — rather than a company union aligned with the studios — gave it greater legitimacy in these negotiations than a studio-aligned body would have had. However, the UAC's influence was limited by the financial power of the foreign conglomerates and by the declining economic conditions of the industry.

The [[1994 Balboa spot recession|1994 spot recession]] was a watershed for the UAC. The bankruptcy of more than half of the operating mainstream studios reduced the UAC's membership and its revenue base, and the 1994 awards ceremony was held in a reduced format. The ceremony that year was noted for a sombre tone that reflected the devastation of the industry, and the Best Picture award was given to a small independent production rather than a studio film, a symbolic recognition of the collapse of the mainstream industry.

=== Post-recession era (1994–present) ===

The post-recession UAC was a smaller, more cautious institution than its Gilded age predecessor. The consolidation of the industry into two major studios — ESC and PTB — and the closure of the smaller production companies reduced the UAC's membership to fewer than 500 by the late 1990s. The awards ceremony was scaled back and held in smaller venues, and the categories were reduced.

The UAC's role began to shift in the 2000s. The gradual recovery of the Balboan film industry, driven by the growth of television production and the emergence of a new generation of independent filmmakers, expanded the UAC's membership and restored the scale of the awards ceremony. The UAC also began to recognise the underground film traditions of the Gilded age: Montserrat Riera i Sola was honoured with a retrospective in 2002, and Kwame Rovira was admitted to the UAC as a member in 2005. The Lifetime Achievement Award, introduced in 1996, became the union's most prestigious honour, recognising figures who had made significant contributions to Balboan cinema over the course of their careers.

The UAC's relationship with the underground filmmakers of the Gilded age has been a subject of ongoing reassessment. The union's exclusion of the underground during the Gilded age has been criticised as a reflection of the mainstream industry's conservatism, and the post-recession admissions of Riera, Rovira, and other underground figures have been regarded as a belated recognition of their contributions. The UAC has responded to these criticisms by expanding its membership criteria to include filmmakers whose work was distributed outside the mainstream theatre system and by establishing a retrospective awards process for films that were not eligible during their original release.

== Awards ==

{{main|UAC Awards}}

The UAC Awards ({{lang|ca|Premis UAC}}) are the principal cinema awards in Balboa, presented annually by the Unió d'Artesans del Cinema since 1983. The awards recognise artistic and technical achievement in Balboan filmmaking across a range of categories. The awards are voted on by the UAC's membership — the creative workers of the Balboan film industry — and the results reflect the professional community's assessment of artistic achievement rather than the studios' commercial priorities.

